zc on OS-9 -- what was changed==============================ZipCode Version 1.0, January 1995, by Joel Mathew Hegberg.  Give it a USpostal zip code and it names the city and state; with no argument it opens asmall full-screen form you can type codes into until you quit.ORIG/ holds the release as it came: zc.c, the author's readme, and hiszipcode.doc, which ships as DOC/zc/zipcode.doc.THE DATA FILE IS THE PROGRAM'S BULK.  zc opens /dd/SYS/zipcodes.txt by thatexact path, and it is 780,496 bytes -- one line per code, tab separated:    00401  Pleasantville  NY    00501  Holtsville     NYIt arrived CR-terminated from the author's own archive and is shipped exactlyas it was, unpacked with this disk's `lha'.  (`lharc' cannot read that archive-- "Sorry, Cannot Extract this method" -- which is worth knowing, since thetwo are different programs and only one of them opens it.)ONE CHANGE, and it removes a key rather than altering an answer.The `Y' key copied the city and state to what the author called the GlobalOS-9 Clipboard, through store_clip_text() from his cliplib.l.  His own linein the source says `(cliplib.l required to compile)', and DOC/zc/zipcode.docdescribes the clipboard as "a proposal in the works".  That library is on nodisk here and the clipboard it belongs to never arrived, so there was nothingto copy into.  The call now sits behind `#ifdef HAVE_CLIPLIB', which nothingdefines, and the key rings the terminal bell -- which is what it already didwhen the field was empty, so it answers instead of doing nothing.  Everyother key is untouched.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  TERM and TERMCAP, which SYS/login sets, andSYS/zipcodes.txt.  The K-Windows twin `kzc' is not here: it wants a displaythis collection cannot offer.